=======================================================================
 WHAT IS HERE, AND WHERE IT GOES
=======================================================================

Four directories, one for each root of the type tree. Every type in
the library descends from exactly one of them, and that is what
decides which directory it lives in.

A file is named for the type it declares. Its module is named for
its path, with the slash written as an underscore, because Fortran
does not allow a module and a type to share a name. So
operation/stencil.f90 holds module operation_stencil, which declares
type stencil.

An abstract type is written graph_something. A concrete type is the
bare noun. That is the only difference in form between them, and it
exists because several abstracts and their concretes would otherwise
collide: graph_field and field, graph_support and support.

